# Create a custom login portal

A login portal is a browser UI for Trellis-owned authentication and consent
flows. It can use any frontend framework. The login flow uses HTTP browser
helpers; it does **not** require an already authenticated NATS client or a
portal participant selecting administrative Auth RPCs.

Device activation and other connected portal features are separate. They use an
app participant and generated APIs; see [devices](/guides/devices). Do not give
the login page administrative authority just to display a login form.

## Register the portal

First verify login through the built-in portal on a running Trellis instance.
Then host your portal on a stable HTTPS URL, for example
`https://login.example.com/login`.

In Console, open **Admin / Portals**, create the portal with that entry URL, and
configure its login providers and registration policy. Add a login route for the
requesting application's origin and/or participant, or select a default portal.
An app's origin selects a route; the registered portal's entry-URL origin is the
trusted portal security principal. Registering that origin is an administrative
trust decision, not a cosmetic redirect setting.

For automation, the same administration is exposed through generated
`Auth.Portals.Put`, `Auth.Portals.LoginSettings.Update`, and
`Auth.Portals.Routes.Put` RPCs. They require appropriate administrator
authority, idempotency keys, and expected versions on updates. Use [/api](/api)
for their current request models rather than issuing those calls from the public
login UI.

## Load the existing flow

The requesting app starts authentication through its Trellis browser client.
Trellis redirects it to your registered portal with `flowId`. Preserve that
identifier; a custom portal must not create a replacement browser flow.

The following TypeScript runs in the browser, after installing
`@qlever-llc/trellis`:

```ts
import {
  fetchPortalFlowState,
  getOrCreatePortalBinding,
  portalFlowIdFromUrl,
  portalProviderLoginUrl,
  portalRedirectLocation,
  submitPortalApproval,
} from "@qlever-llc/trellis/auth/browser";

const config = { authUrl: "https://trellis.example.com" };
const flowId = portalFlowIdFromUrl(new URL(location.href));
if (!flowId) {
  throw new Error("Missing Trellis flowId; start login from the application.");
}
const binding = await getOrCreatePortalBinding(flowId, sessionStorage);
let state = await fetchPortalFlowState(config, flowId, binding);
```

Use your configured Trellis URL, not a URL supplied by an arbitrary query
parameter. The binding is the browser's private per-flow verifier; keep it in
browser storage and never put its secret in a redirect URL or logs. Reloading
the page must recover the same binding. `Origin` and `flowId` alone are not
proof of browser possession.

## Login, consent, and return

Render according to the returned `state.status`:

| Status              | Portal behavior                                                                            |
| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `choose_provider`   | Show only the returned providers and registration options.                                 |
| `approval_required` | Show the requesting app, required authority, and optional bundles; offer approve and deny. |
| `redirect`          | Navigate to the runtime-provided location.                                                 |
| `approval_denied`   | Explain the denial and offer the returned application location, if present.                |
| `expired`           | Explain expiry and return to the application to start a fresh flow.                        |

For a selected OIDC provider from `state.providers`, navigate in the same
browser:

```ts
if (state.status === "choose_provider") {
  const provider = state.providers.find((entry) => entry.id !== "local");
  // Run in the matching provider button's click handler, not automatically.
  if (provider) {
    location.assign(
      portalProviderLoginUrl(config, provider.id, flowId, binding),
    );
  }
}
```

The digest in that URL is not the binding secret. Trellis owns the OIDC
callback, provider verification, and replay cookies. Fetch the flow again after
returning to the portal. For local password login and registration, follow the
built-in portal's account-flow implementation under `web/src/routes/login`; the
Svelte portal controller below does not implement password forms. The OIDC link
above is not a local-password form. Do not proxy credentials to a made-up token
endpoint.

Only in response to the user's consent action:

```ts
state = await submitPortalApproval(config, flowId, binding, "approved", []);
const next = portalRedirectLocation(state);
if (next) location.assign(next);
```

Use `"denied"` for the deny action. An empty optional-bundle list selects
required authority only; otherwise send IDs from the returned optional bundles.
The helper binds approval to the current consent-view digest. Do not construct
new grant sets or broaden the proposal in the UI. Surface typed HTTP errors and
expiry to the user instead of silently starting another approval attempt.

For Svelte, `createPortalFlow({ authUrl, getUrl })` from
`@qlever-llc/trellis-svelte` wraps this flow with reactive state, provider URLs,
approval, and denial. Call `load()` in the browser and use
`portalRedirectLocation(controller.state)` when a state supplies a return URL.
The checked-in browser entry `web/src/lib/portal_login.ts` shows that
integration. See the [Svelte guide](/guides/writing-sveltekit-apps) and
generated API docs for its methods.

## Verify with a real browser

Use your configured portal with a real requesting app. Check successful OIDC
login, explicit denial, expiry, reload recovery, optional-bundle selection, and
return to the requesting app. If enabling local accounts, also check password
login and registration under that portal's configured policy. Keep TLS, allowed
origins, callback URLs, and proxy routing consistent. A compiled snippet alone
does not prove cookies, CORS, or portal policy are configured correctly.
